# SYNOPSIS

```swift
enum Supply {
  static var endingSignal: UInt32 { get }
  static var endedSignal: UInt32 { get }

  static func create(resource: borrowing Handle) throws(Status) -> Handle
  static func bind(_ supply: borrowing Handle, _ object: borrowing Handle)
    throws(Status)
  static func end(_ supply: borrowing Handle, deadline: UInt64)
    throws(Status)
}
```

# DESCRIPTION

A supply stands for what powers a device.
The provider that controls it (the power service for a power domain or a clock, the device manager for an ACPI power resource, a reset controller's driver) holds the supply; the device's driver holds what is bound to it.

`create` makes a supply for the device whose registers `resource` covers, an MMIO resource of a range of pages (`supply_create(2)`).
`bind` binds a physical VMO inside that range, an interrupt object or a BTI to the supply; each handle needs write, and the object is usable only while every supply it is bound to is live (`supply_bind(2)`).

`end` ends the supply and returns when it has ended.
Each bound object asserts `endingSignal`, which a driver waits on with its port and answers by quiescing the device, unmapping its window and closing its handles.
The holders have until `deadline`, an absolute monotonic time in nanoseconds, to do so; then what they still hold is taken: the window's mappings are removed, the interrupts masked, the BTIs' domains aborted.
The supply then asserts `endedSignal`, and only then may the provider cut the power.
A driver that reads on afterwards takes a page fault, not an SError (`supply_end(2)`).

